"""Assistant (level 3): enrich a person from LinkedIn and the web (see assistant.py for the ladder)."""

from app.assistants.base import AssistantSpec, register
from app.assistants.enrich.assistant import ASSISTANT, make_deps, run_item
from app.assistants.enrich.budget import describe, item_cap
from app.assistants.enrich.models import EnrichOptions

DETAILS = """\
**What it does.** For each person, in a fixed order with free fallbacks: resolve the LinkedIn profile, read the person's own website(s), summarise a known GitHub account, resolve and describe the current company, file everything prose- or tag-like the sources returned through the storage agent, then review the record's identity data against what is now on it. Identifiers and our own tools' outputs are written directly; the rest (about text, current title, work history, education, skills, languages, the site's one-liner) goes into one labelled passage so it lands in the right properties with restatements confirmed and conflicts marked. Every write is an appended history entry attributed to the step that made it (enrich/linkedin_reader, enrich/site_reader, enrich/github, enrich/company, enrich/storage).

**Starting it.** One person, or everyone with a `linkedin` value. Options: `refresh` (false: re-run people enriched recently), `max_credits` (1500: the Firecrawl cap for the whole run), `max_new_properties` (0: how many properties the storage agent may create per person for facts the catalog has no home for; at 0 such facts are dropped and reported, never filed under notes) `providers` (["people-data-labs", "fullenrich"]: the data providers asked about the LinkedIn URL, in order; the first match is the profile the later steps rest on, every further match adds its facts to the passage. `people-data-labs` is person/enrich at 1 credit per match: about, title, work history with dates, education, skills, interests, languages; `fullenrich` is people/match at 5 credits: the same minus interests, plus the employer's own description and website. Their records overlapped almost entirely in live runs, so PDL goes first) and `provider_policy` ("gaps": the next provider is asked only when the one before had no record or no about text, about one person in eight; "all": every provider is asked and list facts are unioned). `python -m app.cli providers --alexandria` lists the catalogue's people-data tools with live prices.

**Budget per person.** At most 4 page scrapes plus one data-tool call per provider asked and one for GitHub, 1 web search and 60 credits. Each item reserves that worst case while it runs, so the run can overshoot its cap by at most one item; when the cap is reached the run pauses (reason "credit cap") and Resume with a higher cap continues it. An empty Firecrawl account pauses the run with reason "account credits". The storage step costs one model call and no credits; it is skipped when nothing was gathered or when the same passage was filed before.

**Step 0, the TTL.** A person with a finished enrichment in the last 90 days is skipped for free unless `refresh` is set. Steps that found nothing last time (no provider record, company unresolved) are not retried within the TTL either.

**Step 1, LinkedIn (the data providers via Firecrawl Alexandria; People Data Labs 1 credit per matched profile, FullEnrich 5; a miss is free).** Needs a linkedin.com/in URL on the record. The providers are asked in order under `provider_policy`; the first match is the profile the direct writes and later steps rest on, every further match adds its facts to the passage (a fact two providers state keeps the first text; list values are unioned). The whole paid record is used. Directly: the profile's headline becomes `linkedin_headline`; its GitHub link becomes `github`; `location` is filled when the record has none; `name` is filled when the record's name is missing or a single word the profile's full name contains ("Alina" → "Alina Kharina"; any other mismatch is left to the repair tool); `company` is appended when the profile names a different current employer (the old one stays in history). Into the passage, for step 5: the about text (`about`), the current title (`title_enriched`), the work history as one clause per position with years (`experience`), education (`education`), `skills` and `languages`. An item redone after an interruption skips this step when both the headline entry and the filed passage are already on the record; interrupted between the two, the lookup is paid once more so the facts get filed.

**Step 2, the person's sites (1 credit per page).** Candidates are the profile's personal sites plus every website ever on the record, minus social networks. Up to 2 are scraped (Firecrawl reuses a page cached within 2 days); the site reader agent decides whether the page is the person's own site, and may follow one link. A personal site is appended to `website` when new; its GitHub and related links are collected, and its one-sentence description of the person goes into the passage (`one_liner`). A company or unrelated site is skipped and its links ignored.

**Step 3, GitHub and links (github-com data tool, 5 credits).** The GitHub account from the profile, the pages or the record is summarised into `github_summary` (repos, followers, languages); the account's blog field becomes `website` when it is a personal site not yet on the record, and its location fills `location` when the record has none. Already-summarised accounts are not paid for again unless `refresh`. Related links (newsletters, Twitter and the like, never LinkedIn) are merged into `related_links`, at most 50.

**Step 4, the company (up to 2 pages at 1 credit, one web search at 2 credits; free when the provider describes it).** The current company comes from the profile or the record. When the matched profile carries the employer's own description (FullEnrich does), that description and the employer's website are written directly and no page is fetched. Otherwise the cheapest plausible website is tried first: the provider's, else a website on the record whose host names the company; without one the company agent may search once. Low confidence writes nothing. Otherwise the employer's site goes to `company_website` when new (the person's own sites stay in `website`), `company_description` is written when the record has none or the company website is new, and `company` is filled when the record had none. The evidence URL and confidence stay on the run item, not on the person.

**Step 5, storage (one model call, no credits).** The passage is labelled lines, one per property, in a section per source ("# LinkedIn profile (fullenrich/people/match)", "# Personal site … (site_reader)"); a key stated by two sources keeps one line (list values are unioned, a second text value is dropped and reported). The storage agent files each line into its property in the type's form, confirms values the record already has, marks a different value as a conflict (the old one stays in history), may extract further facts the about text states outright (an email address, a personal website, a GitHub account, investor yes), and may create up to `max_new_properties` new properties for facts with no home; beyond that they are dropped and listed on the item. Identity and employer properties the direct writes decide (name, company, linkedin, linkedin_headline, location, record_kind, company_description, github_summary, related_links) are locked: the agent may confirm them but never derives them from the passage. Provider dumps never reach `notes`. The passage as sent is written to `enrichment_passage` (a text property that is not embedded) in the same commit, so every version sits in the history next to the facts it produced; a `refresh` whose passage equals the record's current `enrichment_passage` makes no storage call. A storage failure leaves the item done with storage "error"; the facts wait for the next `refresh`.

**Step 6, the review (the closing step every assistant shares; at most one model call per agent, no credits).** When the ladder wrote anything, or the record has no kind yet: the parser confirms the typed values the ladder wrote, the normaliser agent reads what the parser cannot, and the repair tool judges the record as it now stands: the kind by common sense (a human name, even a first name alone, is a Person; Unidentifiable only when nothing on the record names a human or an organisation), a first-and-last name the record's own data now states verbatim (a headline or about text the ladder just filed), mechanical fixes, misfiled values. Entries are attributed to enrich/normalizer and enrich/repair.

**Result.** One line per person ("LinkedIn profile matched; GitHub account summarised; company website resolved and summarised; facts filed through the storage agent; wrote about, experience, github, …"), every change, the skipped steps with their reasons, the passage and its hash, `stored` (property → source and the passage words it rests on), `confirmed`, `providers`, the credits and the paid calls, and a Logfire trace per person.
"""

SPEC = register(AssistantSpec(
    key=ASSISTANT,
    label="Enrich from LinkedIn and the web",
    description=("Resolves the person's LinkedIn profile through FullEnrich (Firecrawl "
                 "Alexandria, 5 credits per match), reads their personal site(s), summarises a "
                 "known GitHub account (github-com data tool, 5 credits), records headline, "
                 "current company, websites, related links and a short description of the company "
                 "from its website, and files the profile's about text, title, work history, "
                 "education, skills and languages through the storage agent. Every step has a "
                 "free fallback; nothing is searched for when the record gives no lead."),
    selector_field="linkedin",
    details=DETAILS,
    options_model=EnrichOptions,
    make_deps=make_deps,
    run_item=run_item,
    item_cap=item_cap,
    describe_cost=describe,
))
